# xv6-riscv Extended: 10 Additional Kernel Features

**Course:** Operating Systems
**Instructor:** Sir Raza
**Team:** Muhammad Owais, Muhammad Mudassir, Muhammad Shoaib
**Base:** https://github.com/mit-pdos/xv6-riscv

## 1. Project Overview
This project extends the xv6-riscv teaching kernel with 10 new features across
scheduling, memory management, the file system, and the system call interface.
Each feature includes a user-level test program that demonstrates it.

## 2. Proposed Features

| # | Feature | Description | Owner | Difficulty |
|---|---------|-------------|-------|------------|
| 1 | Priority scheduler | Processes get a priority; `nice(n)` changes it; scheduler picks the highest priority | A | Medium |
| 2 | MLFQ scheduler | Multiple queues with time slices and priority boost to prevent starvation | A | Hard |
| 3 | Lazy allocation | `sbrk` only reserves address space; pages are allocated on first access (page fault) | B | Medium |
| 4 | Copy-on-Write fork | Parent and child share pages until one writes; reference counting for pages | B | Hard |
| 5 | Symbolic links | `symlink(target, path)` syscall, with link following and loop detection | C | Medium-Hard |
| 6 | Large files | Doubly-indirect blocks to raise the max file size | C | Medium-Hard |
| 7 | mmap/munmap | Map files into process memory with VMAs | B | Hard |
| 8 | Kernel threads | `clone`/`join` syscalls plus a user-level thread library | C | Hard |
| 9 | trace syscall | Prints the syscalls made by a process based on a mask | A | Easy-Medium |
| 10 | sysinfo + ps | Reports free memory and process count; `ps` lists PID, name, state | A | Medium |

## 3. Implementation Approach
For each feature: files modified, new syscalls added, user test program,
and how it was tested.

